<section
  id="case-petart-logotype"
  class="fullscreen-section case-study-section"
  data-title="Case Study: Pet Art With Heart — Logo Design | Katja Turnsek"
  data-description="Graphic design case study for a hand-drawn pet portrait identity, developed from pencil sketch into a warm, flexible logo suite."
  data-case="petart"
  aria-labelledby="case-petart-logotype-title"
>
  <div class="rows">
    <div class="row">
      <div class="case-study-header">
        <p class="case-eyebrow">Graphic Design</p>
        <h1 id="case-petart-logotype-title">Pet Art With Heart</h1>
        <p class="case-subtitle">A warm, hand-drawn identity combining pet
          portraiture, fine art, and emotional connection.</p>

        <dl class="case-summary" aria-label="Project summary">
          <div>
            <dt>Role</dt>
            <dd>Brand design and illustration</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Scope</dt>
            <dd>Independent real-world brand</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Status</dt>
            <dd>Ongoing</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Tools</dt>
            <dd>Pencil and paper, Adobe Illustrator</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Outcome</dt>
            <dd>Applied brand identity</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>

        <div class="tech-demo-wrapper case-demo-wrapper">
          <div
            class="demo-links"
            data-auto="demo"
            data-case="petart-logotype"
          ></div>
        </div>

        <nav
          class="project-switcher"
          aria-label="Pet Art With Heart sections"
          data-auto="switcher"
          data-case="petart"
        ></nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="row">
      <h2 class="case-section-heading">The brief</h2>
      <p>
        The identity needed to express the care behind commissioned pet
        portraits while still feeling established and professional. I wanted one
        distinctive symbol that could connect pets, painting, and the word
        “heart,” then work consistently across the website, social profiles, and
        supporting brand material.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="row">
      <div class="case-study-grid">
        <article class="case-study-block case-study-block--challenge">
          <h2>Challenge</h2>
          <p>
            Combining a dog, a cat, a heart, and an artist's palette could
            easily become too literal or detailed. The main challenge was
            reducing those ideas into a soft, memorable silhouette that remains
            readable in small and monochrome applications.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Communicate both pet companionship and artistic craft</li>
            <li>Feel emotional without becoming overly playful</li>
            <li>Preserve the hand-drawn character during vector refinement</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class="case-study-item case-study-media--explore">
          <img
            class="thumb"
            src="{{base}}assets/images/logo-sketch-petart-900.webp"
            data-src="logo-sketch-petart-900.webp"
            data-path="assets/images"
            width="900"
            height="597"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            alt="Pet Art With Heart pencil sketch beside the refined vector symbol of a dog and cat forming a heart and artist palette"
          />
          <figcaption class="case-caption">From pencil sketch to simplified
            vector symbol</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <article class="case-study-block case-study-block--decisions">
          <h2>Key decisions</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Used the animals' curved silhouettes and shared negative space to
              form a heart rather than adding a separate heart shape
            </li>
            <li>
              Integrated palette holes into the cat form so the art reference is
              present without needing another standalone symbol
            </li>
            <li>
              Paired the organic icon with a classic serif wordmark to balance
              warmth with trust and longevity
            </li>
            <li>
              Prepared horizontal, vertical, icon-only, black-and-white, and
              reversed variations for flexible use across media
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class="case-study-item case-study-media--booking">
          <img
            class="thumb"
            src="{{base}}assets/images/design-petartlogosuite-600.webp"
            data-src="design-petartlogosuite-600.webp"
            data-path="assets/images"
            width="600"
            height="423"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            alt="Pet Art With Heart logo suite showing primary, secondary, icon, vertical, monochrome, and reversed variations"
          />
          <figcaption class="case-caption">Logo suite prepared for web, social,
            and monochrome use</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="row case-outcome">
      <h2 class="case-section-heading">Outcome and reflection</h2>
      <p>
        The final identity connects my fine-art background with a practical
        brand system. Its recognisable icon gives the website and commission
        journey a consistent first impression, while the lockups make it
        adaptable enough for everything from a small profile image to a wider
        page header. The project shows how an expressive hand sketch can retain
        its personality after careful digital simplification.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="back-to-work-container">
      <a
        href="{{base}}#work"
        class="label-link back-to-work-label"
        data-back
        data-back-fallback="{{base}}#work"
        aria-label="Back to Work"
      >
        <svg class="icon icon-18 arrow-icon" aria-hidden="true"><use
            href="#i-arrow-left"
          /></svg>
        <span class="label-text">Back to Work</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>